Kind: ablation
Issue: #699
Takes out: U/S decided by the address in pmap_protect_page_noflush() -- a protect clears WRITE, USER and NX together and sets back only what pmap_flags_for_prot() gives, which never carries USER, so every pmap_protect() or pmap_page_protect() of a user page leaves it supervisor-only, as it was from #407 to 6be1b242; protect_user_selftest() reads the leaf after each and must say U/S cleared, and ring3_selftest()'s read of its protected data page must take a page fault on the read; pure software, so TCG shows it as well as KVM
Run: --entry 0 300 -smp 4
Expect: a user page protected read-only reads .* — WRONG, U/S cleared
Expect: ring 3 reading its data page after a protect to read-only took page fault .* — WRONG, the read faulted

diff --git a/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/pmap/map.c b/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/pmap/map.c
index 6f5cfbe0..45321248 100644
--- a/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/pmap/map.c
+++ b/uros/src/mach_kernel/x86_64/pmap/map.c
@@ -523,18 +523,7 @@ uint64_t pmap_protect_page_noflush(pmap_t pmap, uint64_t va, uint64_t flags)
 	 * pmap_pte_update() (pte.h); this was a copy of it, and the copy
 	 * wrote the permission bits into an entry a removal had just zeroed
 	 * (#604).
-	 *
-	 * 🔴 AND THE ADDRESS DECIDES U/S, HERE AS IN pmap_enter() (#699).  The
-	 * permission bits are cleared as a group -- INTEL_PTE_PERM is WRITE,
-	 * USER and NX -- and set back from `flags', which pmap_flags_for_prot()
-	 * builds from a vm_prot_t and so never carries USER.  Every protect of
-	 * a user page took ring 3's access away: the task's next touch faulted,
-	 * vm_fault mapped the page again with U/S, and until then SMAP did not
-	 * guard a page the kernel could reach without asking.  A kernel address
-	 * gets no USER here, as pmap_enter() gives it none.
 	 */
-	if (va_is_user(va))
-		flags |= INTEL_PTE_USER;
 	pmap_pte_update(entry, flags & INTEL_PTE_PERM, INTEL_PTE_PERM);
 
 	pmap_read_leave(held);
